IMPORT prelude
IMPORT daydate
IMPORT `sg-core`
IMPORT `sg-periods`
IMPORT `sga3-76b`
IMPORT `sga3-76c-76e`
IMPORT `sga3-76f-76g`
IMPORT `sga3-76h-76k`

-- Companies Act 1967 (Singapore), 2020 Revised Edition, SSO informal consolidation "version in force from 6/5/2026 to 20/10/2026":
-- the procedure by which a company buys back its own shares (ss 76B to 76K), as one chain of steps laid over the Boolean encodings in sga3-76b.l4, sga3-76c-76e.l4, sga3-76f-76g.l4 and sga3-76h-76k.l4.
-- The chain: an authority (an equal access scheme under s 76C, a selective off-market agreement under s 76D, or a market purchase under s 76E);
-- the lodging with the Registrar of a copy of the resolution within 30 days after it is passed (s 76B(9)(a)) and, in parallel, the purchase within the authority;
-- the purchase, which s 76B(9)(c) dates from the day the company would become entitled to exercise the rights attached to the shares;
-- the notice of purchase within 30 days after that day (s 76B(9)(b)) and the reduction of capital or profits where the shares are cancelled (s 76G(1));
-- and, for treasury shares, a public company's cancellation or disposal at any time (s 76K(1C)) followed by the directors' notice within 30 days (s 76K(1D)), and the disposal or cancellation of the excess within 6 months (s 76I(3)) followed by the same notice.
-- The gates are the existing section functions, called and not restated; where a gate does not answer JUST TRUE (or, for a bar, JUST FALSE) nothing is owed and the rule is FULFILLED at once.
-- Convention for the procedure layer (assumed, not ruled): BREACH is kept only for a duty or a prohibition that the text lays on a named party; a power that lapses unused, a condition that is not met, a vote that fails, a route that has closed and an act the text calls unlawful but forbids no one end FULFILLED.
-- The duty of s 76G(1) has no number of days in the text, so it takes the found period of the section module as a rule-level input (decided, assumed, not ruled).
-- The parties are the corpus's own `Party` (sg-core.l4). "The directors of the company" are one party, `Director "D"`, standing for all of them.
-- HOW THE CLOCKS RUN. A step that follows another through HENCE counts from the day the earlier step happened, because L4 starts a HENCE rule's WITHIN at the earlier event.
-- The resolution is day 0 of each route; the lodging of its copy (30 days from day 0) and the purchase window (from day 0 to the expiry date) run together (RAND);
-- the notice of purchase follows the purchase through HENCE, so its 30 days run from the purchase day, not from the resolution.
-- The clocks of s 76D(7)(a) ("not less than 15 days ending with the date of the meeting") run backwards from the meeting and are checked by the existing gate from two DATE inputs; they are not a step of the trace.
-- Not encoded: the 20% limit of s 76B(3) to (3E) (a numeric gate with no step of its own, answered by sga3-76b.l4), the notice contents of ss 76C(2), 76D(3) and 76E(2) other than the expiry date,
-- the variation and release of an agreement (s 76D(5), (9), (12)), contingent purchase contracts (s 76DA), s 76J (no step and no time), s 76K(1) to (1B), (2), (3) and (4) (powers and bars with no clock).

DECLARE `share buy-back act` IS ONE OF
    `s 76C(1) the company in general meeting authorises the equal access scheme in advance`
    `s 76D(2) pass the special resolution authorising the terms of the agreement`
    `s 76E(1) the company in general meeting authorises the market purchase in advance`
    `s 76B(9)(c) the company becomes entitled to exercise the rights attached to the shares, so that they are purchased or acquired`
    `s 76B(7) lodge a copy of the resolution and a notice of purchase or acquisition with the Registrar`
    `s 76B(9)(a) lodge a copy of the resolution with the Registrar`
    `s 76B(9)(b) lodge a notice of purchase or acquisition in the prescribed form with the Registrar`
    `s 76E(1) make a purchase or acquisition of the company's own shares on a securities exchange`
    `s 76F(3) approve or authorise the purchase or acquisition of the company's own shares or the release of obligations`
    `s 76G(1) reduce the share capital or the profits by the total amount of the purchase price`
    `s 76I(3) dispose of or cancel the excess shares in accordance with section 76K`
    `s 76K(1C) the public company cancels or disposes of the treasury shares`
    `s 76K(1D) lodge a prescribed notice of the cancellation or disposal of treasury shares with the Registrar together with the prescribed fee`

§ `Sections 76B(9)(b) and 76G(1): after the purchase or acquisition`

-- TIMELINE: unit=calendar day; day 0=the day the shares are deemed purchased or acquired, the day the company would, apart from section 76B(5), become entitled to exercise the rights attached to them (s 76B(9)(c)); counting="within 30 days after the purchase or acquisition of the shares" for the notice, the integer line being the count of days with the last day day 30 (sg-periods P1, P2: the day of the purchase is not counted); s 76G(1) puts no time on the reduction, so no clock is encoded for it
-- FORK: s 76B(9)(b) | "the directors of the company" are one party, Director "D", who lodges for all of them, and a public company's failure is a BREACH of that party | a breach of each director separately
-- FORK: s 76G(1) | the text states no time for the reduction, so the period is the found input of the section module (sga3-76f-76g.l4:268-286, Interpretation Act 1965 s 52: all convenient speed; decided, assumed, not ruled; see notes/lest-without-deadline.md), counted from the purchase, and a late or missing reduction is a BREACH by the company | a MUST with no WITHIN and no breach path, or a time taken from the 30 days of s 76B(9)(b)
-- FORK: s 76G(1) | the duty arises only where the shares are cancelled under s 76B(5) and not held in treasury under s 76H, which the caller states in one input | the duty arises on every purchase
-- src:8459 | (9) Where a public company purchases or acquires shares issued by
-- src:8460 | it under section 76C, 76D, 76DA or 76E —
-- src:8467 | (b) within 30 days after the purchase or acquisition of the
-- src:8468 | shares, the directors of the company must lodge a notice of
-- src:8469 | purchase or acquisition in the prescribed form with the
-- src:8470 | following particulars:
@ref s 76B(9)(b) the notice of purchase or acquisition (src:8459-8470)
-- src:9019 | 76G.—(1) Where under section 76C, 76D, 76DA or 76E, shares of
-- src:9020 | a company are purchased or acquired, and cancelled under
-- src:9021 | section 76B(5), the company must —
-- src:9026 | (a) reduce the amount of its share capital where the shares
-- src:9027 | were purchased or acquired out of the capital of the
-- src:9028 | company;
-- src:9030 | (b) reduce the amount of its profits where the shares were
-- src:9031 | purchased or acquired out of the profits of the company; or
-- src:9033 | (c) reduce the amount of its share capital and profits
-- src:9034 | proportionately where the shares were purchased or
-- src:9035 | acquired out of both the capital and the profits of the
-- src:9036 | company,
-- src:9038 | by the total amount of the purchase price paid by the company for the
-- src:9039 | shares cancelled.
@ref s 76G(1) the reduction of capital or profits on cancellation (src:9019-9039)
GIVEN company IS A Party
      directors IS A Party
      `the company is a public company` IS A BOOLEAN
      `the shares are cancelled under section 76B(5) and not held in treasury under section 76H` IS A BOOLEAN
      `the period of all convenient speed, as found` IS A NUMBER @desc The days that make up all convenient speed for this duty, found by whoever applies the rule; there is no default (Interpretation Act 1965 s 52)
GIVETH A DEONTIC Party `share buy-back act`
`s 76B(9)(b) and s 76G(1) after the purchase the directors of a public company lodge the notice of purchase and the company reduces its share capital or profits` MEANS
    IF `the company is a public company`
    THEN ((PARTY directors
           MUST `s 76B(9)(b) lodge a notice of purchase or acquisition in the prescribed form with the Registrar`
           WITHIN 30
           HENCE FULFILLED
           LEST BREACH BY directors BECAUSE "the directors of the public company did not lodge the notice of purchase or acquisition within 30 days after the purchase or acquisition of the shares")
          RAND (IF `the shares are cancelled under section 76B(5) and not held in treasury under section 76H`
                THEN (PARTY company MUST `s 76G(1) reduce the share capital or the profits by the total amount of the purchase price` WITHIN `the period of all convenient speed, as found` HENCE FULFILLED LEST BREACH BY company BECAUSE "the company did not reduce its share capital or its profits by the total amount of the purchase price paid for the shares cancelled (s 76G(1))")
                ELSE FULFILLED))
    ELSE (IF `the shares are cancelled under section 76B(5) and not held in treasury under section 76H`
          THEN (PARTY company MUST `s 76G(1) reduce the share capital or the profits by the total amount of the purchase price` WITHIN `the period of all convenient speed, as found` HENCE FULFILLED LEST BREACH BY company BECAUSE "the company did not reduce its share capital or its profits by the total amount of the purchase price paid for the shares cancelled (s 76G(1))")
          ELSE FULFILLED)

§ `Sections 76B(7), 76B(9)(c) and 76F: the purchase within the authority`

-- TIMELINE: unit=calendar day; day 0=the day the resolution authorising the purchase is passed; counting="a date on which the authority is to expire" (ss 76C(2)(c), 76D(3)(a), 76E(2)(c)): the purchase may be made on any day up to and including the expiry date, so n is the days from day 0 to the expiry date, computed from the two DATE inputs by DATE_SERIAL by the caller; a public company's purchase is the day it becomes entitled to exercise the rights (s 76B(9)(c)), a private company's is its lodging (s 76B(7)); the solvency of s 76F(4) is tested at the date of the payment, which the caller states
-- FORK: s 76B(7) | for a private company the lodging is the step by which the purchase is made, so the permitted act is the lodging and no clock runs from it | the lodging as a duty after a separate purchase
-- FORK: s 76B(9)(c) | a public company's purchase is the day it becomes entitled to exercise the rights, because (c) deems the shares purchased then | the day the agreement is made
-- FORK: s 76C(2)(c) | the permission to purchase lapses at the expiry date with nothing owed (LEST FULFILLED) | a purchase after expiry as a BREACH
-- FORK: s 76F(2)(a) | where the company is not solvent the very act the permission names (becoming entitled, for a public company; lodging, for a private one) is barred, and doing it ends FULFILLED with 'the purchase is not lawful' beside it, because the text says only that no purchase is lawful and lays no duty on a named party | a BREACH by the company, as the rule stood before the review
